* START-HISTORY:
* 12 Oct 26 dm  New.
* 28 Oct 26 dm  S.88 L1 (R-a): a resize that a dialogue read (and the program never saw) is the key this statement gives first, before it waits;
*               a resize it reads itself clears the note, so that it is given once.
* 3 Nov 26 dm  A key name the toolkit's menu bar left for the program (common /$slkpush/: the choice made in a box's right-click menu that opened after the click moved the
*               focus, $tk_menubar) is given first, once, before any pending resize and before the keyboard is read.  It is a name the program would get for that key.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   SCREEN KEY {TIMEOUT s} RETURNING k {THEN ... ELSE ...}
*
*   is compiled into
*
*   call !screen_key(timeout, ret, status)
*
*     timeout  seconds to wait; left out, for ever
*     ret      the key's name: up down left right home end pgup pgdn ins del bs tab btab enter esc
*              f1 to f12, ctrl-a to ctrl-z (those with no name of their own), or the character itself.
*              The terminal's own terminfo entry decides what a sequence means ($sl_key).  A report of the
*              mouse (once SCREEN OPEN has switched reporting on) is the name mouse: SCREEN MOUSE then says
*              what it was.  resize when the terminal's size changed - also when it changed while a dialogue (a menu,
*              a message box, SCREEN ASK ...) was open, which then had the key: the program is given it at its next
*              SCREEN KEY, at once and before any key typed since.
*     status   0 a key; 1 the time ran out or the input has ended (ELSE)
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine screen_key(timeout, ret, status)
$catalog !screen_key

   common /$slkpush/ kp.name                       ;* 3 Nov 26: a $ name, so no user program can declare it (CLAUDE.md: a common SD trusts has a $ name); 'kp:' and a key name the toolkit's menu bar left for this wait (a common starts as 0, so the prefix marks a real one)

   ret = ''
   if kp.name[1, 3] = 'kp:' then
      ret = kp.name[4, 999]
      kp.name = ''
      status = 0
      return
   end
   call $sl_scr('pending', pend)                   ;* a resize a dialogue read and the program did not see (R-a): it is the key, first
   if pend then
      ret = 'resize'
   end else
      call $sl_key(timeout, ret)
      if ret = 'resize' then call $sl_scr('pending', pend)    ;* delivered here: the note is not left for the next call
   end
   if ret = 'resize' then
      call $sl_scr('get', scr)                 ;* makes the kept picture the terminal's size and draws it again
      call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   end
   status = if ret = '' then 1 else 0

   return
end

* END-CODE
